  "difficulty": "INTERMEDIATE",
  "season": null,
  "sourceType": "CLASSIC",
  "sourceNotes": "Simit has been sold in Istanbul since at least the 16th century; the Ottoman court accounts mention simit sellers in the 1525 Kanun-name. The bread is also made in Levantine countries (sesame-coated ka'ak or ka'ak al-eid in Lebanon and Palestine share the same form). The defining preparation technique, dipping in pekmez (grape or mulberry molasses) before seeding, is documented in Turkish culinary encyclopaedias including Süheyl Ünver's research on Ottoman foods and in Ayla Algar's Classical Turkish Cooking (1991).",
